def rob(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        if len(nums) == 1:\n            return nums[0]\n\n        def linear(houses):\n            skip, take = 0, 0\n            for x in houses:\n                skip, take = max(skip, take), skip + x\n            return max(skip, take)\n\n        return max(linear(nums[1:]), linear(nums[:-1]))\n","approach":"**Break the circle by cases: the first house is skipped, or the last house is skipped (at least one must be).** Each case is an ordinary linear street, so the answer is `max(rob(nums[1:]), rob(nums[:-1]))` using the House Robber recurrence `best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + nums[i])` with two rolling variables.\n\n**Why it works:** the two subproblems cover every legal circular plan (a plan robbing both ends is illegal; any other plan omits an end and is legal in the corresponding linear street), and every linear plan is legal on the circle because the dangerous pair is never both present.\n\n**Common mistakes:** the n = 1 case, where both slices are empty and the code would return 0 instead of nums[0]; forgetting that for n = 2 the answer is simply the max.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Reconstruct the chosen houses? (Keep the DP arrays for both runs and backtrack in the winning one; shift indices by one for the nums[1:] run.) Could you do it in one pass? (Carry two pairs of rolling variables simultaneously, one per case.) What if no two houses within distance k may be robbed? (Sliding-window max over best(i-k-1 .. ) with a deque.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["house-robber","house-robber-iii"],"number":125,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def rob(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}